From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 17:27:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: view whole cubes when a cube-array layer-face needs
 one

vrend_create_surface() gives a colour attachment its own texture view
whenever the surface's format differs from the resource's, which is how a
guest renders into an sRGB texture with GL_FRAMEBUFFER_SRGB off: Mesa asks
for the linear format of the same resource, and vrend answers with
glTextureView(). For GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP_ARRAY it passed the one layer-face
the attachment names, and a cube-array view may not be one layer-face:
numlayers must be a multiple of 6, so the call is GL_INVALID_VALUE and the
generated texture keeps no storage at all. The attachment is then a texture
that was never specified, every draw into that layer-face is lost, and the
guest reads the layer back as it was left.

dEQP-GL45-ES31.functional.fbo.color.texcubearray.srgb8_alpha8 is the case
that shows it. sglr::ReferenceContext turns its sRGB update off for a GL core
context, so the test expects the unconverted write a desktop context makes,
Mesa asks for PIPE_FORMAT_R8G8B8A8_UNORM over the PIPE_FORMAT_R8G8B8A8_SRGB
resource, and vrend logs twelve "Create texture view" lines - one per
layer-face - that all fail. The same case on an ES context passes, because
there sRGB update is always on, the surface format equals the resource format
and no view is asked for; and the non-sRGB formats of the same group pass for
the same reason. A host probe against this stack confirms the rule rather
than the driver: a cube-array view of 1 layer is GL_INVALID_VALUE at minlayer
0 and at minlayer 7, while 6 or 12 layers are accepted and complete as an
attachment.

View the whole texture instead. A cube array always holds a whole number of
cubes, so numlayers is legal, and starting at layer 0 keeps
glFramebufferTextureLayer's index meaning what the surface says it means -
exactly what the GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP case beside it already does with its six
faces.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
index 625843bd1eb08489cde196eb1fa2c41b4b174963..e054b58333fb818ca4c80be53ba7bfbacd9c245b 100644
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c
@@ -2413,6 +2413,15 @@ int vrend_create_surface(struct vrend_context *ctx,
          if (target == GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP && first_layer == last_layer) {
             first_layer = 0;
             last_layer = 5;
+         } else if (target == GL_TEXTURE_CUBE_MAP_ARRAY &&
+                    first_layer == last_layer) {
+            /* A cube-array view must cover whole cubes, so one layer-face
+             * cannot be viewed on its own, and the attachment below still
+             * names the layer-face by its index in the resource, so the view
+             * has to start at layer 0 for that index to keep its meaning.
+             * Both are what the cube-map case above does. */
+            first_layer = 0;
+            last_layer = util_max_layer(&res->base, surf->level);
          }
 
          int num_layers = last_layer - first_layer + 1;
